              <p class="text-[#16A34A] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Buyer's Guide</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">Best Deck Stains</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">Which gallons earn their premium, tested through a full year.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Budget $2 to $5 per square foot for a professional job</strong> — about $900 to $1,600 on a typical 300 square foot deck with railings — or $180 to $300 in materials if you are doing it yourself. Most contractors hold a minimum charge of $400 to $600, so small decks pay a disproportionate share of setup.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The condition of the deck moves the number more than its size does. A wash-and-coat deck sits at the bottom of every band; a deck that needs stripping and sanding first can cost twice as much. Run the water test in several places before the estimators arrive, and you will know which quote is describing your deck honestly.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Judge the result on cost per year of protection. The cheapest one-coat quote usually buys the fewest years and is the most expensive way to own a deck over a decade — while recoating on schedule, before the finish fails, is the only saving here that never comes back to bite.
          </p>
        </div>


